    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Cat Says 'Meow'.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay messages from teacher to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret teacher cues and relay sound information (e.g., the teacher says, 'The cat says meow,' and the student says 'meow' to a peer). Can describe and mimic common animal sounds to peers, using simple words, phrases, or gestures (e.g., says 'meow' and points to a picture of a cat when asked what sound a cat makes). Can assist peers in identifying sounds (e.g., points to a picture of a dog after hearing 'woof').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Animal Sound Circle Time." Students sit in a circle. The teacher shows animal flashcards (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep) one by one and asks, "What animal is this?" and "What sound does it say?" Students respond chorally, making the animal sounds (e.g., "Meow!", "Woof!"). The teacher emphasizes pronunciation of /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the act of relaying information to a peer. The teacher says, "Listen! I hear a 'meow'." (teacher whispers 'meow' to one student). "Now, you tell your friend 'meow'!" The teacher encourages the student to turn to a peer and whisper/say 'meow'. The teacher then points to the cat flashcard. This demonstrates how to relay simple sound information. The teacher also explicitly points out the letter 'C' in 'cat' and 'D' in 'dog' on the flashcards, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the cow say 'quack'?" (No!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the duck say 'moo'?" (No!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me the animal that says 'woof'?" (Students point to the dog flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio track of various animal sounds (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, pig, horse). Large, clear picture cards of these animals. Teacher introduces new letters P, Q, R by showing cards with these letters and relating them to animals like 'Pig' (P), 'Quack' (Q for duck sound), 'Rooster' (R).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing and describing common animal sounds, identifying animals based on their sounds (Pragmatic and Sociolinguistic Competence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a segment of the animal sound audio. After each sound, the teacher asks, "What sound is this?" and "What animal says [sound]?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students chorally repeat the sound and try to identify the animal. The teacher holds up the corresponding picture card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then states sentences using present simple: "The cat says meow," "The dog says woof," "The rooster says cock-a-doodle-doo."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to mimic the sounds and gestures (e.g., flap arms for 'bird,' make a big circle for 'cow').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the animal that says 'oink!'" (Students point to the pig picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: The sheep says 'quack'." (False!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> WH-questions ('What sound is this?'), Present simple ('The pig says oink.'). Exclamations ('That's a rooster!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, fish, pig; say; loud, quiet, big, small; bark, meow, quack, woof.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practicing /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation:</b> Relaying simple sounds and descriptions from teacher to peer or peer to peer.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "Teacher Says, You Relay!" (Controlled practice):</b> Students sit in pairs. The teacher calls out an animal sound (e.g., "Woof!"). Student A hears the sound and then turns to Student B and repeats ("woof!"). Student B then points to the dog picture on their desk. The teacher ensures students are relaying the sound and not just identifying it themselves.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Whisper Chain" (Semi-controlled substitution drill):</b> Students form small lines or circles. The teacher whispers an animal name (e.g., "cow") or sound ("moo") to the first student. That student then whispers the animal name or sound to the next student. The last student in the chain says the sound aloud and points to the corresponding animal flashcard that the teacher holds up. This activity encourages careful listening and accurate relaying.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Sound and Describe Match" (Pair interaction/Peer check):</b> Students work in pairs, each with a set of mixed animal flashcards. Student A picks a flashcard (without showing it to Student B) and either makes its sound or says a simple description (e.g., "It's big, and it says moo!"). Student B listens to the sound/description (mediating the information) and tries to guess the animal and point to the correct flashcard. They can ask, "What sound is this?" or "Is it a cow?". Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially for /m/ and /r/, and reinforcing the use of "What sound is this?".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Animal Sound Guessing Game" – directly implementing the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group has a set of animal flashcards spread on the table.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from each group is designated the "Sound Listener" for the round. The teacher secretly shows an animal flashcard to the "Sound Listener" (e.g., a pig).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Sound Listener" then makes the sound of that animal (e.g., "oink, oink!") to their group. They are mediating the sound information from the teacher to their peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students in the group listen to the sound (interpreting the mediated cue), discuss ("What sound is this?"), and then collaborate to find the correct animal flashcard and point to it. They might exclaim, "That's a pig!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher ensures students are using the target grammar ("What sound is this?," "The pig says oink.") and vocabulary (loud, small, big, quiet) to describe and guess.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns being the "Sound Listener" and mediating the information to their peers. The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of sounds and letters (like 'P' for pig) and encourages gestures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Animal Sound Guessing Game." The teacher focuses on individual student's ability to relay and interpret information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Animal Sound Guessing Game," specifically demonstrating their ability to:
                    <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relay the animal sound accurately (as the "Sound Listener").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpret the relayed sound information from a peer to identify the animal.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Assist peers in identifying animals, using simple words or gestures.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can relay common animal sounds clearly (e.g., "meow," "woof") to a peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple words or gestures to describe the animal or sound if needed (e.g., points, says "big cow").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively assists peers in identifying animals based on mediated sounds/descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates collaborative engagement in the game.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "What sound is this?" or "What animal is this?" appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you help your friend guess the animal today?" (Students give a thumbs up for "yes" or thumbs down for "no".)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What was your favorite sound to share?" (Students make their favorite animal sound.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest animal sound to share with your friends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new sound did you learn or share today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in fostering mediation skills for Kinder students? The "Whisper Chain" and "Sound Listener" roles effectively encouraged relaying simple information. Students demonstrated good ability to mimic and interpret sounds. Some students needed more encouragement to verbalize the animal name (e.g., "It's a cat!") after hearing the sound, relying more on pointing. I will need to provide more sentence starters like "It's a..." for the quieter students. The integration of phonemic awareness with letters P, Q, R was successful when associated with the animal pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "The Cat Says 'Meow'." The next lesson will transition to a new theme, possibly exploring "My Favorite Food" or "Colors Everywhere," building on vocabulary acquisition and simple sentence structures for Kinder students in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>